From afde617fc5c9fd3c783ce870f994ba71be91bcf5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 18:48:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] sdk: build the vendored SDK on stock mainline

The vendored NXP DPAA SDK drivers (sdk_fman, sdk_dpaa, staging/fsl_qbman)
target the NXP LSDK kernel, which carries wiring and APIs that stock
mainline lacks. Bundle every change needed to build the SDK overlay on a
stock kernel.org tree into one ASK-owned patch; each consumer that bases
on mainline (rather than the NXP vendor tag) needs it. Folds five
previously-separate OpenWrt build-compat patches (705/710/711/712/713).

Driver wiring (ex-705):
 - source the sdk_fman/sdk_dpaa Kconfigs and add their obj-$() entries
   under drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/; source + wire staging/fsl_qbman
   (the qbman/bman/usdpaa driver, CONFIG_FSL_SDK_DPA). This is the
   keystone that activates the vendored drivers.

Mainline API shims (ex-710):
 - ioremap_cache_ns(): bman/qman portal CE cacheable maps. mainline has no
   *_ns (PROT_NORMAL_NS) variant; NS is irrelevant on the non-secure
   LS1046, so map it onto ioremap_cache (cacheable Normal memory).
 - pgprot_cached()/pgprot_cached_ns(): usdpaa mmap uses them for cacheable,
   non-shareable userspace portal/buffer maps. mainline arm64 has neither.
 - phylink_interface_max_speed(): the sdk_dpaa MAC driver calls it;
   mainline keeps it static. Un-static + export.
 - skb_recycle(): dropped from mainline, kept by NXP and used by sdk_dpaa's
   Tx recycle path. Restore it verbatim from the NXP tree.

SDK source alignment (sdk_dpaa/mac.c):
 - the MAC driver's sole reference to PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_2500SGMII -- an
   enum name the NXP kernel carries but mainline lacks -- is renamed to
   PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_2500BASEX, mainline's name for the 2.5G SerDes mode.
   The board DT declares "2500base-x", so this matches what of_get_phy_mode()
   delivers; the sole use is a full-duplex-only if_support mask, so it is
   behaviourally identical. Renaming the reference (rather than #define-ing
   the missing name in a header) is the collision-free form: OpenWrt's 702
   adds PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_2500SGMII as a real enum member, so a same-named
   macro in this patch would collapse 702's switch cases onto the 2500BASEX
   cases (duplicate-case build error) once a 702-carrying tree consumes it.

Warning hygiene (ex-711):
 - mark dpa_get_bp_device() __maybe_unused; it is only called from the SEC
   inbound path, compiled out when CPE_FAST_PATH is disabled, so an
   SDK-only (fast-path-off) build would otherwise trip
   -Werror=unused-function.

Board support (ex-712):
 - swphy: decode a 10000 Mbit/s fixed-link like 1000 (no distinct 10G MII
   code). The Mono Gateway's 10G (xfi) MACs use fixed-link 10000; mainline
   swphy returns -EINVAL and the MAC fails to probe.

Export for the ppp hook (ex-713):
 - export rtmsg_ifinfo(): ASK patch 070's PPP fast-path hook calls it from
   ppp_generic; mainline dropped the export after its last in-tree caller
   went away, so a modular ppp_generic fails modpost. CPE_FAST_PATH=n
   builds compile the hook out, so this only matters for the ASK image.

NOTE (phylink vs OpenWrt 702): the phylink hunk here only un-statics the
phylink_interface_max_speed() signature and appends EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL after
the function's closing brace. OpenWrt's 702 (2.5G-SGMII, deliberately not
imported here) edits the *interior* of the same function -- it adds a
PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_2500SGMII switch case -- plus phylink_get_capabilities().
The two edit disjoint regions and must stay that way: a consumer carrying
both 702 and this patch must keep 702's edits inside the switch body and
this patch's edits at the signature/export lines, so the hunks never
overlap when both are applied.

Signed-off-by: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
